/.quarto/
/_book/
**/*.quarto_ipynb

# Rendered HTML never belongs in git. `quarto render` writes the book into
# `_book/` (ignored above), so nothing next to a `.qmd` is a source file.
#
# Thirty chapters were nonetheless committed in `cf468ec` as top-level
# `book/*.html` — byte-identical duplicates of the ignored `_book/` output. The
# exact tool invocation that produced them was not established (a plain
# `quarto render marks/zone.qmd` writes to `_book/` like the full build does), so
# this rule guards the *outcome* rather than a guess at the cause: whatever puts
# an `.html` beside a `.qmd`, git will not see it.
#
# They behaved like a slow leak. A full render *deletes* them, so every render
# produced 30 spurious deletions, which invited copying them back — preserving
# the accident instead of ending it. The set was never even complete:
# `grammar.html` and `marks/index.html` were never tracked at all, and
# `marks/path.html` was added in one commit and deleted in the next.
#
# If a hand-written `.html` include is ever needed here, un-ignore it explicitly
# (`!partials/foo.html`) rather than loosening this.
*.html

# The one hand-written HTML here, un-ignored by name as the rule above asks. It
# is `include-after-body`, and it colors a gog specification by kind: a table, a
# mark, a channel, a transform, and one quiet ink for the six that refine. The
# blog stages a copy, the direction `gog.css` and the palette already travel.
#
# It fails quietly if it is missing. The book still builds, exits 0, and simply
# prints every specification in one ink, which looks like a decision rather than
# a loss. That is why it is named here rather than trusted to be noticed.
!/gog-syntax.html

# The PDF build's figure files. `knit_print.gog_spec` writes each plot's SVG and
# its converted PDF into knitr's own `fig.path`, which for a book chapter is
# `<chapter>_files/figure-pdf/` — beside the `.qmd`, not inside `_book/`. They are
# generated on every PDF render and are worth nothing in git: ~48 files for one
# chapter, both formats of each plot.
#
# Ignored as an *outcome* rule for the same reason `*.html` above is. The first PDF
# build put 48 of them into a commit that was about a stylesheet, which is precisely
# how `cf468ec` started — artifacts entering a commit whose subject was something
# else, then being deleted by the next render and invited back.
*_files/

# The PDF build's LaTeX intermediates. `quarto render --to pdf` assembles the whole
# book into `index.tex` beside the `.qmd`s and runs lualatex there, so `index.aux`,
# `index.log`, `index.toc` and an intermediate `index.pdf` appear next to
# `index.qmd`. A *successful* render tidies them away, which is why they went
# unnoticed for as long as they did — but a render that **fails or is interrupted**
# leaves them, and a full PDF build takes long enough that a `git add -A` during one
# is an ordinary thing to do. That happened on 2026-07-26: a render was interrupted
# by an edit to a chapter it was already rendering, and five untracked `book/index.*`
# files were sitting in `git status` a moment later.
#
# The same outcome rule as the two above, and named individually rather than as
# `index.*` so that `index.qmd` — the actual source file, whose name they all borrow —
# cannot be caught by a widened glob.
/index.tex
/index.aux
/index.log
/index.toc
/index.pdf

# knitr's intermediate markdown, written beside the `.qmd` it came from. A
# successful render removes it, so it only shows up after one is interrupted —
# the same way the LaTeX intermediates above do, and it wants the same rule.
*.knit.md

# The PDF render's own intermediate, and the same rule again. Quarto writes a
# `.rmarkdown` beside each chapter on the way to LaTeX and deletes it when the
# render succeeds, so like the two above it is only ever seen after one is
# interrupted. It was seen: `marks/step.rmarkdown` sat in `git status` during a
# full build and was gone a minute later. A render takes long enough that
# reading `git status` in the middle of one is an ordinary thing to do, which is
# how the other outcome rules here were earned.
*.rmarkdown

# `publish.sh` tees the render log here so it can be graded afterwards. Quarto
# exits 0 with broken links, so the log is the only evidence a render went well.
/.publish-render.log

# R's fallback graphics device. Base R opens `Rplots.pdf` in the working
# directory the first time anything draws with no device already open, and a
# full book render is a long non-interactive R session, so one appears beside
# `_quarto.yml` afterwards. Four kilobytes of nothing, regenerated by the next
# render, and therefore exactly what the outcome rules above exist to keep out
# of a commit whose subject is something else.
/Rplots.pdf

# The browser engine and its runtime, copied in by `R/setup.R` at render time so
# Quarto can put them in `_book/` beside the pages that fetch them. Build output
# of a cargo invocation, not source — the same outcome rule as everything above,
# and named here because `book/` is otherwise an allowlisted public directory
# where a stray 823 KB binary would simply be committed.
/gog.wasm
/interactive.js
/view.js
